---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Research — Junwon Lee"
  description="Research areas: diffusion LLM inference, KV-cache compression and quantization, speculative decoding, and video diffusion efficiency — where algorithms meet the memory wall."
>
  <!-- hero -->
  <section class="section hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
      <h1>
        The cost of inference is <em>bytes moved</em>,<br />
        not math done.
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Modern language-model inference rarely runs out of arithmetic — it runs out of
        memory bandwidth. Weights and KV caches stream through HBM far slower than tensor
        cores can consume them, so the useful question is almost never "how do we compute
        faster?" but "how do we move less?" I study training-free ways to answer that for
        diffusion LLMs and their neighbors: the interesting problems live at the boundary
        of algorithms and hardware (알고리즘과 하드웨어의 경계), where a decoding rule,
        a cache layout, and a roofline plot all constrain each other.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- research areas -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research areas</p>
      <h2>Four rooms, one wall</h2>
      <p class="lede areas-lede">
        Different model families, same recurring bottleneck: memory movement.
        Each area below is a different angle on that one wall.
      </p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card area-card area-dllm">
          <span class="card-kicker">01 · Diffusion LLMs</span>
          <h3>Diffusion LLM inference</h3>
          <p>
            Diffusion LLMs decode many tokens per step instead of one, which rewrites the
            serving economics we take for granted in autoregressive models. I work on
            parallel decoding schedules, what can safely be cached across denoising steps,
            and measuring dLLM inference by end-to-end serving cost and quality —
            not per-step proxies.
          </p>
          <span class="card-meta">
            <span class="tag">parallel decoding</span>
            <span class="tag">caching</span>
            <span class="tag">serving economics</span>
            <span class="tag">dLLM vs AR</span>
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="card area-card area-kv">
          <span class="card-kicker">02 · Memory</span>
          <h3>KV-cache compression &amp; quantization</h3>
          <p>
            At long context and large batch, the KV cache — not the weights — is what
            caps how many requests fit on a GPU. I study low-bit and structured
            representations of keys and values that shrink that footprint while keeping
            accuracy, and why keys and values deserve different treatment in the first place.
          </p>
          <span class="card-meta">
            <span class="tag">low-bit KV</span>
            <span class="tag">quantization</span>
            <span class="tag">long context</span>
            <span class="tag">memory wall</span>
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="card area-card area-spec">
          <span class="card-kicker">03 · Decoding</span>
          <h3>Speculative &amp; parallel decoding</h3>
          <p>
            Speculation trades cheap drafts for expensive verifications, and the whole
            bargain hinges on acceptance behavior. I care about the latency structure
            underneath: when drafting pays for itself, how acceptance rates move with the
            task, and how these ideas compose with parallel decoders rather than compete
            with them.
          </p>
          <span class="card-meta">
            <span class="tag">drafting</span>
            <span class="tag">acceptance rates</span>
            <span class="tag">latency structure</span>
          </span>
        </div>

        <div class="card area-card area-video">
          <span class="card-kicker">04 · Multimodal</span>
          <h3>Video &amp; multimodal diffusion efficiency</h3>
          <p>
            Video diffusion transformers hit the same memory-wall story with the volume
            turned up: enormous attention states, heavy redundancy across denoising steps
            and frames. The open question is which text-side lessons transfer — and which
            quietly fail — when the tokens are spatiotemporal.
          </p>
          <span class="card-meta">
            <span class="tag">video DiT</span>
            <span class="tag">attention caching</span>
            <span class="tag">cross-domain transfer</span>
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- how I work -->
  <section class="section band-dark work-band">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">How I work</p>
      <h2>Three habits I try not to break</h2>
      <div class="principles">
        <div class="principle">
          <span class="principle-num">1</span>
          <h3>Observe before you optimize.</h3>
          <p>
            A bottleneck is an empirical fact, not an assumption. Before touching any
            technique, profile where the bytes and the milliseconds actually go — the
            measured system is allowed to disagree with the paper diagram, and usually does.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <span class="principle-num">2</span>
          <h3>A method must be forced by a mechanism.</h3>
          <p>
            A good method is the corollary of an explanation: first understand why the
            cost arises, then let the fix follow from that understanding. If a simpler
            baseline would work, the honest move is to use it and say so.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="principle">
          <span class="principle-num">3</span>
          <h3>Report what the measurement says.</h3>
          <p>
            Matched baselines, controlled comparisons, and numbers taken from runs that
            actually finished. Negative results are results — an experiment that rules
            out a plausible story earns its keep.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- publications -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Publications &amp; preprints</p>
      <h2>Selected work</h2>

      <div class="pub-empty">
        <p class="serif pub-empty-line">First entries are in the pipeline.</p>
        <p class="pub-empty-note">
          Until they land here, the writing lives elsewhere: I keep a running set of
          reviews of other people's papers — dLLMs, KV caching, sparse attention —
          on the Paper Review page.
        </p>
        <a class="arrow-link" href="/reviews/">Read the paper reviews</a>
      </div>

      <!-- template: copy one <li class="pub-item"> per publication once entries exist.
      <ul class="pub-list">
        <li class="pub-item">
          <span class="pub-venue tag accent">VENUE 2027</span>
          <h3 class="pub-title"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/XXXX.XXXXX">Paper Title Goes Here</a></h3>
          <p class="pub-authors"><strong>Junwon Lee</strong>, Coauthor One, Coauthor Two</p>
          <p class="pub-blurb">One-sentence description of the contribution.</p>
          <span class="card-meta">
            <a class="arrow-link" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/XXXX.XXXXX">arXiv</a>
            <a class="arrow-link" href="https://github.com/Romaanj/repo">Code</a>
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      -->
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 6.5rem);
  }
  .hero h1 em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--accent-strong);
  }
  .hero .lede {
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
  }

  .areas-lede {
    margin: 0.9rem 0 2.2rem;
  }

  /* area cards: swatch-tinted top rule per area */
  .area-card {
    border-top: 3px solid var(--line-strong);
  }
  .area-dllm  { border-top-color: var(--accent); }
  .area-kv    { border-top-color: var(--swatch-slate); }
  .area-spec  { border-top-color: var(--swatch-olive); }
  .area-video { border-top-color: var(--swatch-sky); }

  /* how I work band */
  .work-band h2 {
    margin-bottom: 2.4rem;
  }
  .principles {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
  }
  .principle {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-on-dark);
    padding-top: 1.3rem;
  }
  .principle-num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }
  .principle h3 {
    font-size: 1.22rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .principle p {
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* publications empty state */
  .pub-empty {
    margin-top: 1.8rem;
    max-width: var(--prose-width);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 2rem);
  }
  .pub-empty-line {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.4vw, 1.8rem);
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .pub-empty-note {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    max-width: 40em;
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
  }
</style>
